    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d430">

                <pb facs="0326" n="276" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav03/pg276"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.77/518</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Morris</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-10-23T17:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 23,
                                1918, 5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Referring to Siberian railways. If you can clear up the point in doubt in Article
                    2 of the memorandum of agreement, Department desires you to emphasize the fact
                    that Mr. Stevens, and the Russian Railway Service Corps which will assist him,
                    will represent Russia and not the United States or any possible interest of the
                    United States. The Department considers this point essential because the United
                    States has no desire or purpose to secure an interest in the railways of Russia
                    or to control them for itself.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
